  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\n...TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY...\n\nUpper air analysis shows that the much talked about mid/upper ridge \nhas shifted southwest slightly and is now primarily centered \nover New Mexico early this morning. Meanwhile, a subtle \nshortwave trough is rounding the northern periphery of this \nridge, while mid-level WAA is overspreading portions of \nnorthern Kansas and southern Nebraska. As a result, there is \nenough ascent to overcome the very warm mid-level temperatures, \nand scattered showers and storms are ongoing across central and \nnorthern Kansas early this morning. Subtle mid-level WAA should \ncontinue through the morning hours along the mid-level \nbaroclinic zone, and this should support continued shower and \nthunderstorm activity across central, northern, and eastern \nKansas through the morning hours. Like mentioned previously, \nmid-level temperatures remain very warm (greater than 28 C at \n850 mb), which will keep a lid on the intensity of this activity.\nThus, severe weather is not expected; however, brief heavy \nrainfall, gusty winds, and occasional lightning strikes are \npossible.\n\nAssuming the coverage of showers and storms today remains along and \nnorth of US-50, much of southern and southeast Kansas will continue \nto feel the presence of the mid/upper ridge. Afternoon \ntemperatures around 100 and dewpoints in the upper 60s to low \n70s will generally support heat indices around 105 south of \nUS-50 this afternoon into the early evening. \n\nThe forecast from late tonight through Friday will generally be a \nrinse and repeat each day. Starting with tonight, another very weak \nand subtle shortwave trough will round the north/northeastern \nperiphery of the upper ridge while mid-level WAA overspreads much of \nnorthern and eastern Kansas at the nose of the nocturnal LLJ. This \nwill support another round of widely scattered showers and storms \nacross portions of central, northern, and eastern Kansas late \ntonight through much of Wednesday morning. Similar to this morning, \nmid-level temperatures will remain very warm which should keep the \nintensity of this activity in check. Generally speaking, the main \nconcerns late tonight through Wednesday morning will be limited to \nbrief heavy rainfall, gusty winds, and a few lightning strikes.\n \nThanks to some lingering cloud cover and the aforementioned ridge \ncontinuing to gradually retrograde into the southwestern CONUS, \nWednesday afternoon won't be quite as hot with afternoon highs in \nthe mid to upper 90s and heat indices generally remaining below 105. \nThen, a similar setup to the previous couple of days is expected \nWednesday night into Thursday morning with scattered showers and \nstorms possible across northern, central, and eastern Kansas. \nThursday afternoon will be slightly warmer with some locations \nacross south-central Kansas reaching 100 during the afternoon hours. \nGoing into Thursday night, mid-range guidance suggests a \nstronger shortwave trough will round the northern side of the \nridge and sweep across the Rockies and northern/central plains \nThursday into Friday morning. As a result, more widespread \nshowers and storms are forecast Thursday night into Friday \nmorning across the entire forecast area. This activity is still \nlikely to struggle with very warm mid-level temperatures\nremaining in place across the region. Therefore, severe weather\nis not expected at this time; however, there's still time for \nthings to change, so trends will need to be monitored over the \ncoming days. Some of this activity could linger throughout the \nday on Friday across far eastern Kansas, but in general, storm \nchances will be coming to an end by Friday night.\n\n...SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY...\n\nMid to long range deterministic and ensemble model guidance suggests \nthe upper level pattern will be momentarily locked in for a few days \nthis weekend into early next week with troughing across the eastern \nCONUS, and a strong ridge parked over the Desert Southwest. This \nwill put Kansas awkwardly in the middle of the features with \nnortherly upper flow remaining quite persistent for a few days.\nAt the surface, a series of weak cold fronts appears \nincreasingly likely which should support cooler temperatures \nthis weekend, perhaps 5 to 10 degrees below average for this \ntime of year. Going into early next week, the pattern becomes \nincreasingly ill-defined, but it appears that slight increases \nin heights aloft should support a gradual warming of \ntemperatures through the beginning of next week.",
